{"10061701": "19 Community Drive  Augusta, Maine 04330  (207) 622-7501  Fax: (207) 623-3590\nTestimony in Support of LD 2230 (\"An Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing\")\nJ. Andrew Cashman on behalf of the Maine Association of REALTORS� March 11, 2026\nSenator Curry, Representative Gere and members of the Joint Standing Committee on Housing and Economic Development my name is Andy Cashman. I am the Founder of Resolve Government Relations We represent the Maine Association of REALTORS�, a professional trade association established in 1936 with over 6,500 members statewide. REALTORS� protect private property rights, build Maine communities, and grow our state's economy. Our members represent buyers and sellers involved 0.in both residential and commercial real estate transactions. Our membership also includes industry affiliates, such as lenders, closing agents, title agents, appraisers, building inspectors, surveyors, etc. The Maine Association is chartered by the National Association of REALTORS� (NAR), the largest trade association in the country.\nThe Maine Association of REALTORS� supports LD 2230 because it would encourage the development of more housing types in our state. This bill provides grants or loans to manufacturers of industrialized housing; establishes a construction extension partnership program; and a competitive innovative housing construction pilot program. The passage of this bill would allow for streamlined housing development that is more affordable and requires a shorter time to complete. LD 2230 incentivizes development and addresses a critical component of Maine's housing needs, more supply and less regulation.\nAs stated in our public policy statement, MAR advocates for the adoption of policies that encourage housing and economic development and availability of all housing types including factory-built housing, modular housing, manufactured housing, tiny homes, and other types of housing for ownership or tenancy. We also support innovations in development and construction techniques and LD 2230 would embrace these policies to more effectively increase the production of housing that our communtiies and our workforce need.\nLD 2230 is an example of policy that responds to the housing needs of our communities and we respectfully urge you to vote Ought to Pass. Thank you for your time and consideration.\n- is a registered mark which identifies a professional in real estate who subscribes to a strict Code of Ethics as a member of the NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS�", "198205": "g i Maine Community College System\nOFFICE OF THE PRESIDENT\n323 State Street, Augusta, ME 04330-7131\n(207) 629-4000 I Fax (207)629-4048 I mccs.me.edu\n\nTO:\nFROM:\nRE:\nDATE:\n\nSenator Curry, Representative Gere Joint Standing Committee on Housing and Economic Development\nBecky Smith, Director of Government and Community Relations\nTestimony in Support of portions of LD 2230, An Act to Invest in the Construction of\nIndustrialized Housing March ll, 2026\n\n.,\",>$\"...\"`$fl|1I�, . :; .2. , 1 : ; taivndrbiihil. ;`\n\n;P.-�fl?%1'\n\nt. 2.22%-Iwi f.;._; ._ 1 ; 1&1, J; #2::-=M\n\nSenator Curry, Representative Gere and members of the Joint Standing Committee on Housing and Economic\nDevelopment, I am Becky Smith, Director of Government and Community Relations for the Maine Community\nCollege System and I am here to offer our support of Section 4 of LD 2230, An Act to Invest in the Construction\nofIndustrialized Housing\nThe December 2025 Report of the Housing Production Innovation Working Group, prepared for your committee, identified ways Maine could increase innovative construction technologies and thereby increase capacity and reduce the cost of new housing. The Maine Community College System's colleges are already working towards many of the goals set forth in the report.\n\nWe are pleased to participate in the construction extension partnership program outlined in Section 4 of the bill.\n\nThe Maine Community College System has a long and proud history of meeting industry needs--and industrialized housing would be no exception. Building Construction Technology is taught at four of our\ncolleges and those that may not teach in that specific field, teach HVAC, Plumbing, Heavy Equipment, and\nArchitectural and Engineering Design--all necessary for industrial housing.\n\nCentral Maine Community College is currently exploring enhancements to their Building and Construction program to accommodate the unique needs of industrialized housing. As you can imagine, facilities and equipment will need to be upgraded, and we would need to work closely with the vendors who currently\nprovide industrialized housing on the appropriate curriculum. Every MCCS program is required, by policy, to\nhave a program advisory committee which includes industry, this would be no exception.\n\nOur Alfond Center for the Advancement of Maine's Workforce also provides the Construction Institute----free of charge--with Southern Maine Community College. I have attached a flier to this testimony as just one small\nexample of what we do every day and how we could partner with the industrialized housing businesses in\nMaine. I have also attached this year's program matrix for your information.\n\nThank you for taking the time to listen to our testimony. Thank you.\n\nI\n\nI\n\nPg Maine Communit y\nCollege System\n\nAssociate Degree and\nCertificate Programs\n\nindustrial Technology and Transportation\n\nM Air Conditioning & Refrigeration\n\n'E{>HriEEET�IrbHE�?6Sfi1puier\n\nA/C\n\nI`?,�I`ll`?.'E�Y_.,\nf`5P3EE.'!Hi�iW_ W.-. ._.c_M_. .1'\n\nWM -c__ ~....-_ W __.\n\n.\nC\nNJ-\n\n&L�_'!l'�.��\".'3`�:E\".&'i !i `S�fiB$3�_9�'l__ -Marine Design\n\nAutornotlve_Coi|lsion Repair\n\n>_ _\n\n5iii9mo\";iCi�Ei{iTiiBTg;*\"\"\nV .\n\n:E.�.l,cWsjs.`.e\n\nM\n\nw\n\nWZTA)\nc\nA\nA/C\n\nff..1._'; �;f<��fIIIl��1iLIl'1Iffff\" .,.\n\nl\\4l1\\ )6(PN)()nAMY\n\n,\n_-- n-adr-\nAM\n\n-�.re�ie.?.�isr%?r7�fj17\n\n1\n\nr\">rQ .....