\n(72 hours)\n\nQ WHERE\nSMCC South Portland Campus\nConstruction Technology Building\n65 Fort Road\nPREREQUISITES:\n� At least l8 years ol age\n' High School diploma, GED\nor HiSel\n� Resident at Maine\n� Prool ol authorization to\nwork in the U.S.\nTHANK YOU T0 OUR PARTNER:\niEva FOR THE ADVANCEMENT OF HAROLD ALFOND CENTER Mmulrs wonxroncn", "10061697": "Jeffrey Harmon - Auburn Susan Hawes - Bangor Liam LaFountain - Biddeford Carl Sheline - Lewiston Mark Dion - Portland Adam Lachman - Rockland\n\nJodi MacPhail - Saco Becky Brink - Sanford Elyse Tipton - South Portland Michael Morris - Waterville David Morse - Westbrook\n\nMaine Mayors Coalition Testimony on LD 2230 An Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing\nSenator Curry, Representative Gere and distinguished members of the Joint Standing Committee on Housing and Economic Development, the Maine Mayors Coalition is providing testimony in support of LD 2230, An Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing.\nThe Maine Mayors Coalition is a nonpartisan group representing the mayors of eleven Maine communities - Auburn, Bangor, Biddeford, Lewiston, Portland, Rockland, Saco, Sanford, South Portland, Waterville and Westbrook - and nearly 300,000 residents. The Coalition advocates on behalf of our citizens and municipalities for policies that recognize the vital services Maine cities provide and the positive impact Maine cities have on our state. The Coalition seeks to work in partnership with state and federal elected ofOicials to meet the needs of Maine people.\nAs we have said before, Maine's housing crisis directly impacts each of our cities, and housing is top of mind for all of us. Every day we see the human and economic consequences that come from the lack of housing supply and the ever-rising costs of homes and rents. Our cities continue to work to find ways we can help preserve existing affordable housing and develop new affordable housing. Both are critical for our cities - and our state - to thrive.\nIn our capacity as mayors, we work with community members and partners to foster ideas for housing initiatives and advance creative solutions for families and individuals across all income levels. Incentivizing the development of industrialized housing through the Housing Opportunity Program and a public-private partnership with the industry would offer an additional opportunity for our state to create even more safe, affordable housing.\nThe Maine Mayors Coalition appreciates all the work this Committee and the Legislature has undertaken to help alleviate Maine's housing crisis. We respectfully offer our support of LD 2230 as one more path to continue your efforts.\nThank you for your time.", "198202": "Glenn \"Chip\" Curry\nSenator, District 11\n\nits\n\n-V\n\n...,\n\n,,t .\n\nTHE MAINE SENATE\nl32nd Legislature\n\n3 State House Station Augusta, Maine 04333\n\nTestimony of Senator Chip Curry introducing\nLD 2230, \"An Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing\"\nBefore the Joint Standing Committee on Housing and Economic Development March 11, 2026\nRepresentative Gere and distinguished colleagues of the Joint Standing Committee on Housing and\nEconomic Development, my name is Chip Curry. I represent Senate District 11, which includes all 26\nrural, coastal, and island communities of Waldo County. Thank you for the opportunity to introduce\nLD 2230, \"An Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing.\"\nThis bill comes before the committee pursuant to Resolve 2025, chapter 105, which we are more\nfamiliar with as LD 1453 from the first session of the l32nd Legislature, titled \"Resolve, to Establish\nthe Housing Production Innovation Working Group.\" That legislation established the Housing Production Im1ovation Working Group to study ways innovative housing construction technologies could help reduce housing production costs and accelerate the pace of housing development in Maine.\nAs required by that resolution, the working group submitted its findings and recommendations to this\ncommittee late last year. LD 2230 was reported out by the committee in order to place several of those\nrecommendations into bill form so that the Legislature may hold a public hearing and consider them\nthrough the normal legislative process.\nAmong other things, the bill proposes creating an Industrialized Housing Incentive Program to support\nmanufacturers producing housing units in Maine, establishing a public-private construction extension partnership program to provide technical support and workforce training related to innovative housing construction, and creating a pilot program to encourage efficient construction of multifamily housing\nprojects.\nI'm supportive of moving this bill forward because Maine needs to be exploring every responsible way\nto increase the supply of housing. Innovative construction approaches, such as modular and other forms of industrialized housing, have the potential to reduce costs and shorten construction timelines.\nThe bill also encourages stronger public-private partnerships to connect manufacturers, builders, educators, and workforce programs, so Maine can build the skills and capacity needed to support this\nkind of innovation.\nTogether, these ideas are intended to help Maine test and support new approaches to housing\nconstruction while continuing to build the workforce and industry capacity needed to meet our housing\nneeds. ~\n\nChair, Housing and Economic Development Committee * Criminal Justice and Public Safety Committee State House (207) 287-1515 * Fox (207) 287-1585 * Toll Free 1-800-423-6900 * TTY 711 Chip. Curry@legis1oture. maine. gov * legislature. maine.gov/senate", "198204": "JANET T_ MILLS\nGoveimon\n\nSTATE OF MAINE\nMAINE OFFICE or COMMUNITY AFFAIRS\n127 STATE HOUSE STATION 32 BLOSSOM LANE, 31111 FLOOR souru\nAUGUSTA, MAINE\n04333-0127\n\nSAMANTHTA D. norm\nD\"\"3�T�\"\n\nTESTIMONY BEFORE THE JOINT STANDING COMMITTEE ON HOUSING AND\nECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT\nIN SUPPORT OF LD 2230\n\nAn Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing\n\nMarch ll, 2026\n\nSenator Curry, Representative Gere, and members of the Joint Standing Committee on Housing\nand Economic Development, my name is Hilary Gove. I am one of the two Housing Opportunity Program Coordinators within the Maine Office of Community Affairs (l\\/IOCA). I am speaking today in support of LD 2230, An Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing.\n\nThis bill provides multiple incentives to support the construction of industrialized housing. These incentives were the result of the Housing Production Irmovation Working Group.\n\nSpecific to MOCA, this bill requires the Housing Opportunity Program within MOCA to\nencourage and support the development of housing units, including industrialized housing and housing units that affordable to low~income, moderate-income, and middle-income individuals. It\nrequires MOCA to define low-income, moderate-income, and middle-income in rule, as well as\nprovide information to manufacturers, developers, and the general public about housing needs.\n\nMOCA supports this bill because it incentivizes the construction of industrialized housing in\nMaine by providing financial and technical support to builders of manufactured housing. The Housing Opportunity Program is well situated to provide support to municipalities, developers, and the public about housing needs and resources for increasing housing.\n\nHowever, MOCA recommends removing Sections 2 and 6 of this bill. Section 2 requires MOCA\nto adopt rules to define low-income, moderate-income, and middle-income. Section 6 requires\nMOCA to adopt rules about grant eligibility. The Housing Opportunity Program, now that it is\n\nwithin the Maine Office of Community Affairs, is in the process of repealing its existing grant\neligibility rule. This rule is no longer applicable because MOCA's programs are exempt from the\n\nDepartment of Administrative and Financial Services' statutes governing grants to communities.\n\nThe Housing Opportunity Program defines eligibility for grants and key terms in a program\nstatement. We have not identified a current need within MOCA's program to define income levels,\n\nand so we recommend removing the requirement to do so.\n\nIi`\n\nMNNEOFHCEOF\n\n'\n\n`\n\nQ@m,mfii'l?W\n@fifi�l\"'�\n\nMaiquzudl Bulldmg 3\"` Floor, 32 Blossom Lane, Augusta, Maine 04330\n\nJANET T_ MILLS\nGOVERNOR\n\nSTATE OF MAINE\nMAINE OFFICE o1= COMMUNITY AFFAIRS\n127 STATE HOUSE STATION 32 BLOSSOM LANE, 3RD FLOOR SOUTH\nAUGUSTA, MAINE\n04333-0127\n\nSAMANTHA D. HORN\nDIRECTOR\n\nThank you for your time. I would be happy to answer any questions now or at the work session\n\nG 6\n\n`\n\n'\n\nA\n\n2?\n\n1 A MMNEOFFICEOF `\nQgrnfilfiumty\nfififiw�\n\nM2u'qua|'dt Building, 3\"\" F I001\",\n32 Blossom Lane, Augusta, Maine 04330", "10061686": "Testimony in Opposition to LD 2230: \"An Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing\"\nSenator Curry, Representative Gere, and distinguished members of the Joint Standing Committee on Housing and Economic Development, my name is Montana Towers, and I serve as policy analyst for Maine Policy Institute. Maine Policy is a free market think tank, a nonpartisan, nonprofit organization that advocates for individual liberty and economic freedom in Maine. Thank you for the opportunity to submit testimony in opposition to LD 2230, \"An Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing.\"\nWhile we appreciate the bill's recognition that Maine faces a serious housing shortage, we believe this legislation takes the wrong approach by creating new government programs and taxpayer-funded subsidies instead of removing the real barriers that prevent private innovation from flourishing. Our state needs more housing, built faster and at lower cost, but the solution lies in freeing the market rather than expanding bureaucracy.\nIndustrialized Housing\nThe Maine Policy Institute strongly supports the expanded use of industrialized housing, including modular, panelized, and factory-built systems. These methods have proven their value across the country by dramatically shortening construction timelines, reducing on-site labor costs, and delivering high-quality, energy-efficient homes that meet or exceed traditional building standards. In Maine's challenging climate and regulatory environment, factory-controlled production offers consistency that site-built homes often struggle to achieve.\nWe believe industrialized housing could play a major role in easing our state's housing crisis if allowed to compete on a level playing field with conventional construction. Removing outdated regulatory hurdles would let private manufacturers, builders, and homebuyers drive this innovation forward naturally, without the need for government direction or spending.\nProblems with More Government Programs and Subsidies\nUnfortunately, LD 2230 does not simply promote industrialized housing. Instead, it establishes multiple new government initiatives that rely on taxpayer dollars and expand the role of state agencies. Maine already has one of the highest state and local tax burdens in the nation, and our state budget continues to grow. Adding yet another layer of spending programs, revolving funds, and bureaucratic oversight is precisely the opposite of what is needed.\n\nWhen the government attempts to pick winners through grants, loans, or pilot programs, the result is often distorted markets, higher long-term costs, and benefits that flow disproportionately to politically connected recipients rather than to the families who need housing most. True progress on housing supply comes from reducing the cost and complexity of building, not from transferring wealth from taxpayers to new government programs.\nMore Pilots and Reports\nThe legislation also directs the department to design a public-private construction extension partnership program by January 2027, involving extensive consultation with higher education institutions, labor departments, chambers of commerce, and various industry groups, followed by a formal report and potential new legislation. Additionally, the bill requires the creation of a competitive innovative housing construction pilot program for multifamily projects, complete with financial awards based on cost, speed, and \"innovation,\" plus another report to the Legislature in 2028. Each of these components adds administrative expenses, rulemaking authority, and ongoing oversight that will ultimately be paid for by Maine taxpayers. These programs represent a further expansion of state government into an industry that is already overly regulated and is capable of rapid growth if it is simply left unencumbered unencumbered.\nA Superior, Market-Oriented Path Forward\nTowards that end, if the Legislature genuinely wants to accelerate the adoption of industrialized housing, far more effective and cost-free solutions are available. Maine should work with municipalities to modernize zoning and land-use laws so that higher-density development and factory-built homes can be permitted in appropriate areas. Providing clearer statewide guidance would help reduce the patchwork of local regulations that currently drive up costs and delay housing projects. State building codes and permitting processes should also be streamlined so that modular and panelized systems approved under national standards can be installed without unnecessary duplicative reviews. Barriers that treat industrialized housing differently from traditional site-built homes should be repealed or reformed so the market can determine which methods deliver the best value to consumers.\nThese reforms would unleash private investment, encourage competition among manufacturers, and allow communities to benefit from faster, more affordable housing without creating new spending programs or bureaucratic infrastructure. Permitting reform rather than subsidies would lead to the supply increasing faster and more sustainably when the government steps back rather than steps in.\n\nMPI strongly supports the goal of expanding housing choice through innovative construction methods, but we cannot support legislation that responds to a shortage caused largely by regulation with more government programs and taxpayer spending. Maine families deserve real solutions that lower costs and increase supply through market forces, not through new subsidies and bureaucracy.\nFor these reasons, Maine Policy Institute strongly urges this committee to vote \"Ought Not to Pass\" on LD 2230. Thank you for your time and consideration.", "198203": "\\\\\"` I Ii\np\n\nff\n\n1%,\n\n`~ W -I\nIANET T. MILLS\nGOVERNOR\n\nSTATE or MAINE DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AND COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT\n\nECONOMlC&COMMUNlTY DEVELOPMENT\nMICHAEL DUGUAY\nCOMMISSIONER\n\nMarch 11,2026\nTestimony of Phoenix McLaughlin\nRegarding LD 2230\nAn Act to Invest in the Construction of Industrialized Housing\nSenator Curry, Representative Gere, and members of the Committee on Housing and Economic\nDevelopment, my name is Phoenix McLaughlin, and I am the Director of Strategy Implementation at the Department of Economic and Corrmiunity Development. I am here today to testify on LD 2230.\nThe Housing Production Innovation Working Group, which I staffed, recommended incentivizing the use of industrialized construction in Maine. To quickly summarize the case,\nindustrialized construction methods----meaning greater use of factory-built components, modules, ~\nand homes----can reduce construction costs in the right conditions. One of those conditions is\nadequate scale to unlock the efficiencies of manufacturing. Incentivizing the use of industrialized\nconstruction in Maine can help reach that needed scale, ultimately bringing down per-unit costs\nthrough upfront investment.\nThis bill offers multiple ways to incentive industrialized construction, each of which the working group touched on. The first is to directly provide an incentive for the production and installation of factory-built housing. As proposed, the manufacturer would receive a bonus for housing both produced and installed in Maine. This is modeled most closely on a Colorado program, which supported over 900 units constructed in their state between mid-2023 and fall 2025 and is still\nongoing.\nThe second is to establish a partnership aimed at improving construction methods in Maine. An\nearly theme in the working group's conversations was the need to further the adoption of new\ntechniques in the construction industry. This model is well-proven in the manufacturing and agricultural industries, which have longstanding extension partnership programs in Maine and across the country. This program would provide technical support to builders, improve data\ncollection, and assist in facilitating certification in new building systems, which will be a key\nfunction if manufactured housing regulation is revised as recommended.\n\n59 STATE HOUSE STATION ~ AUGUSTA ~ MAINE - 04333-0059 PHONE: (207) 624-9800 ' FACSIMILE: (207) 287-2681 WWW.MA1NE.GOV/ DECD\n\n.\nit\n\n..\n\n.\n\n` 5'!:'\n\n,t;,,5@E`i;i�_i@�\\Y*l\n\nJANET T. MILLS\ncovzzmon\n\nSTATE OF MAINE DEPARTMENT or ECONOMIC AND COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT\n\nM A N| E ECONOMIC & COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT\nMICHAEL DUGUAY\nCOMMISSIONER\n\nThe third component would establish a prize competition for innovative housing production.\nThis would be a novel program to not only test and evaluate new building systems at scale, but to\nbuild Maine's reputation as a center of housing innovation.\n\nLastly, consumer and municipal education on different housing systems is key to growing the market. The Housing Opportunity Program can be a vehicle to help municipalities and the public understand what is possible in construction.\n\nThere are some changes we would like to see if the committee moves forward with this bill. For\nexample, the Department would recommend offering the incentives strictly through grants, not\nloans. A loan product would not be a good fit for a per-unit incentive like this, and it would require another level of staffing to underwrite loans and track repayment. We are happy offer\nsuggested language for any additional recommended changes before the work session.\nThank you for your time, and I am happy to answer any questions.\n\n59 STATE HOUSE STATION - AUGUSTA - MAINE ~ 04333-0059 PHONE: (207) 624-9800 ~ FACSIMILEI (207) 287-2681 WWW.MAINE.GOV/ DECD"}